# Kernel pin file
#
# The build host reads this file to fetch exactly one Linux source tree.
# It is the only place a kernel version is named.
#
# Exactly ONE uncommented `version=`, `url=`, and `sha256=` line must exist
# below the real values. Everything that is an example is commented out with
# `#` so it can never be mistaken for the active pin.

version=6.12.111
url=https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/linux-6.12.111.tar.xz
sha256=9e59dc67624188fa12a6601f9598499cd6662a9066be572b59f935e3d7849810

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How to fill this in (on the BUILD host, not on the machine that organises
# this repository).
#
# Arm guidance (research/program-scope.md section 4, VERIFIED): for a new
# virtual test environment use the latest Android Common Kernel or the latest
# Linux Kernel stable/longterm release. So pin the newest longterm (LTS)
# release, unless a documented reason picks otherwise.
#
# To discover the current releases and their authoritative checksums:
#
#   curl -s https://www.kernel.org/releases.json
#   curl -s https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/sha256sums.asc
#
# Then replace the three real lines at the top of this file with one exact
# release. Example of the required SHAPE (do not uncomment as-is; the version
# and checksum below are illustrative only and are NOT verified):
#
#   version=6.12.42
#   url=https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/linux-6.12.42.tar.xz
#   sha256=<64-hex value copied from the authoritative sha256sums.asc>
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# - No floating "latest". `url` must name one exact file.
# - `sha256` must be non-empty. fetch-kernel.sh refuses to run without it.
# - Checksum mismatch is fatal; it is never "fixed" by blind re-download.
# - The pinned version is recorded in the artifact manifest and in
#   analysis/kernel-compatibility.md, so an artifact always names its kernel.
# - Changing this file changes the kernel. Do that deliberately and record it.
#
# Compatibility note: r54p0's highest observed KERNEL_VERSION gate is 6.13.0,
# but a gate is NOT a ceiling (see analysis/kernel-compatibility.md). If the
# chosen latest-LTS kernel fails to build Kbase, record the exact first error
# (per research/methodology.md failure categories), then try the next justified
# candidate. Do not silently downgrade.

# --- candidate 1: 6.18.54 — FAILED, do not reuse ---
# resolved 2026-09-30 by kernel/scripts/resolve-kernel-pin.sh (latest LTS)
# moniker=longterm  released=2026-09-25
# tarball directory: https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x
# Failure (VERIFIED, build/baseline, log kept at
# research/build-logs/6.18.54-baseline-FAILED.log):
#   midgard/mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c:83,158 — implicit declaration of
#   __SetPageMovable / __ClearPageMovable.
#   Cause (VERIFIED): those symbols left include/linux/migrate.h in v6.17;
#   they are still declared in v6.12..v6.16. r54p0 calls them unguarded.
#   This is an r54p0-vs-kernel API break, NOT a configuration problem.
#
# --- candidate 2 (CURRENT): 6.12.111 — the newest LTS at or below 6.16 ---
# moniker=longterm  released from the same authoritative sha256sums.asc
# Justification: newest `longterm` release that still exports the symbols
# candidate 1 proved missing. Selected by evidence, not convenience; it is a
# step down from the newest LTS and that step is recorded here on purpose.
# ARM guidance (latest stable/longterm) still applies; the deviation is forced
# by the build result, and is documented in analysis/findings.md F-16.
# checksum source: https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/sha256sums.asc
# ARM guidance: latest ACK or latest stable/longterm (see ../BUILD-HOST.md)
